$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{-3x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3P+2Q=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94030/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94030"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94025/selection/625c7475830be38f9b652344f3989b24bab8e8fb52b8e2b8f49e8b87dbd4a391" :answer/id #uuid "9526f7da-c5a2-5229-8c04-427c82ffec3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94025/selection/1bc231c7f57cd5d06655803c63ed745944076324c7c9f09781d59eac45cf39f6" :answer/id #uuid "809594a4-bc02-5125-a768-1cd7a1cd76f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94025/selection/381edb47cd795e557f3eae0f5cbfd467c83d91c99cb96a7a5528981ab0e84509" :answer/id #uuid "2a3b9c83-5da5-5871-b9b4-783921d31572" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94025/selection/d3f5e5a5ea3011e3f931a068f5e0e3fa4cce69a4632cce1c40633f9ac4f15f8f" :answer/id #uuid "4c32223b-bd89-5532-8b33-6fee0b6f38ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94025/selection/3adeac8e6eb3bc2fe177e065c3a7661a51a8390c986554920fd3240b3a54aa55" :answer/id #uuid "bd55a92c-aaa8-5e2b-8071-d42258bad871" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-2x}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94025/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb40ba8-5d4d-5a46-bfc6-33ec658a4419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94025/selection/1bc231c7f57cd5d06655803c63ed745944076324c7c9f09781d59eac45cf39f6" :answer-field/choices ["q-94025/selection/625c7475830be38f9b652344f3989b24bab8e8fb52b8e2b8f49e8b87dbd4a391" "q-94025/selection/1bc231c7f57cd5d06655803c63ed745944076324c7c9f09781d59eac45cf39f6" "q-94025/selection/381edb47cd795e557f3eae0f5cbfd467c83d91c99cb96a7a5528981ab0e84509" "q-94025/selection/d3f5e5a5ea3011e3f931a068f5e0e3fa4cce69a4632cce1c40633f9ac4f15f8f" "q-94025/selection/3adeac8e6eb3bc2fe177e065c3a7661a51a8390c986554920fd3240b3a54aa55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94025" :question/id #uuid "70c4757b-9d7e-52fc-8fec-97d1c574cab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94025" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-2x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2Q{e}^{-\\pi }-P{e}^{-\\pi } & =6{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -2Q-P & =6 \\\\ -2-P & =6 \\\\ P & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-8$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94025/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94025"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6861"] :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2}=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6862"] :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 4 y' + 4y =0,\n\\qquad y(0) =  3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda = 2,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny'  = 2 A e ^ {2 x} +  B e ^ { 2 x} + 2 B x e ^{ 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 2 & =2A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=A \\\\ 2=2A+B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =6+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4,$ and therefore the solution is \n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ { 2 x } - 4 x e ^ { 2 x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6791"] :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B.$ To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2,$ $y'(\\pi)=-1.$\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2 \\\\ A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  & =2 \\\\ -A & =2 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $y'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\ny' =  - A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ -A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & =-1 \\\\ -B & =-1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7051"] :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2,  \\quad y'(0) = 5, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2 = P.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+{e}^{3x}(-4P\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{e}^{0}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+{e}^{0}(-4(2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 5 & =6+4Q \\\\ -1 & =4Q \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( 2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
